WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.170 line:77%
If Earth’s rotation stopped this instant,
the ground would brake.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.900 line:77%
But you, the air, and the oceans
would keep moving.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.400 line:77%
At the equator, Earth carries you east
at about 1,040 miles an hour.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.660 line:77%
Inertia would make that speed
suddenly matter.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.120 line:77%
That is the immediate answer:
a catastrophic

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.470 line:77%
mismatch between a stopped surface
and a moving world.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.580 line:77%
Here’s the proof hiding under your feet.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.770 line:77%
NOAA explains that the farther
you stand from the equator,

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.450 line:77%
the slower your eastward ride.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.520 line:77%
Near the poles, it approaches zero.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.360 line:77%
At the equator, it reaches roughly
1,040 miles per hour.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.530 line:77%
We don’t feel it because the ground,
buildings, oceans,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.400 line:77%
and atmosphere share that motion.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.000 line:77%
A spinning planet is a moving platform.
